window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.2","title":"p-values and Significance","goal":"Turn the bottling plant's z statistic into one-sided and two-sided p-values, and see what alpha = 0.05 means as a cut-off.","question":"A bottling plant claims its bottles hold 500 mL on average, and the filling machine's spread is known (sigma = 12 mL). A quality inspector weighs 64 bottles and gets a mean of 503.6 mL. Is the plant really overfilling, or could a gap of 3.6 mL just be sampling noise?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"From the data to a z statistic","text":"H0: the true mean fill is 500 mL. H1: it is not 500 mL (over or under). We weighed 64 bottles (sigma = 12 mL known) and got 503.6 mL. The z statistic says how many standard errors the sample mean sits from the claim.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nmu0, sigma, n, xbar = 500, 12, 64, 503.6\n\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nz = (xbar - mu0) / se\n\nprint(f'standard error = {se:.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for the square root and number work, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; stats.norm is the normal curve we need for p-values.","Store the claim (500), the known sigma (12), the sample size (64) and the sample mean (503.6) in one line so every later line can use them.","The standard error: how much a mean of 64 bottles typically wobbles. We divide sigma by the square root of n.","The z statistic: the gap between our mean and the claim, measured in standard errors. Dividing by the SE makes it comparable to the normal curve.","Print the standard error rounded to 2 decimals so we can check it by hand (12 / 8 = 1.5).","Print z, the distance from the claim in standard errors."],"meaning":"The standard error is 1.50 mL, so a mean of 64 bottles typically wobbles about 1.5 mL from the true mean. Our sample mean is z = 2.40 standard errors above the claim. That is a fairly large gap, but z alone is not yet a probability, so next we turn it into a p-value.","out":"standard error = 1.50\nz = 2.40","figs":[]},{"title":"One-sided and two-sided p-values","text":"norm.sf (the 'survival function') gives the area in the right tail beyond z. The two-sided p-value counts both tails, so it is double the one-sided value. A left tail uses norm.cdf.","code":"p_right = stats.norm.sf(z)         # area beyond +z\np_left = stats.norm.cdf(z)         # area below z\np_two = 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z))  # both tails\n\nprint(f'right-tailed p = {p_right:.4f}')\nprint(f'left-tailed p  = {p_left:.4f}')\nprint(f'two-tailed p   = {p_two:.4f}')","explain":["norm.sf (survival function) gives the area of the normal curve beyond z on the right. That is the p-value if H1 says 'more than 500'.","norm.cdf gives the area below z on the left. That is the p-value if H1 says 'less than 500'.","For H1 'not equal to 500' both tails count, so we double the area beyond |z|. abs() makes it work for a negative z too.","Print the right-tailed p-value to 4 decimals.","Print the left-tailed p-value to 4 decimals.","Print the two-tailed p-value, the one that matches our H1 of 'different'."],"meaning":"The two-tailed p is 0.0164. If the true mean were 500 mL, a sample mean at least 2.4 standard errors away in either direction would happen about 1.6% of the time. The right-tailed p (0.0082) is exactly half. The left-tailed p of 0.9918 shows the data point away from 'below 500'. None of these is the chance that the claim is true.","out":"right-tailed p = 0.0082\nleft-tailed p  = 0.9918\ntwo-tailed p   = 0.0164","figs":[]},{"title":"Alpha as a cut-off (critical values)","text":"Alpha = 0.05 is the surprise threshold. norm.ppf turns it into critical z values that cut off the rejection region. Comparing |z| with the critical value gives the same decision as comparing p with alpha.","code":"alpha = 0.05\ncrit_two = stats.norm.ppf(1 - alpha / 2)   # two-tailed cut-off\ncrit_right = stats.norm.ppf(1 - alpha)     # right-tailed cut-off\n\nprint(f'two-tailed critical z = +/-{crit_two:.3f}')\nprint(f'right-tailed critical z = {crit_right:.3f}')\nprint('p <= alpha?     ', p_two <= alpha)\nprint('|z| > critical?', abs(z) > crit_two)","explain":["Choose the surprise threshold alpha = 0.05 before judging the data, so we cannot move the goal posts.","norm.ppf is the inverse of the curve: the z value that leaves alpha/2 in each tail. This gives the two-tailed cut-off.","For a one-sided test all of alpha sits in one tail, so the right-tailed cut-off is the z with 5% above it.","Print the two-tailed critical value; any z beyond plus or minus this is in the rejection region.","Print the right-tailed critical value.","Rule 1: reject H0 when the p-value is at or below alpha. This prints True or False.","Rule 2: reject H0 when |z| is beyond the critical value. It must agree with rule 1."],"meaning":"The two-tailed critical value is 1.960 (and 1.645 for a right-tailed test). Our |z| of 2.40 is beyond 1.96, and p = 0.0164 is below 0.05, so both rules print True and both say reject H0. The two rules always agree, so use whichever is handier.","out":"two-tailed critical z = +/-1.960\nright-tailed critical z = 1.645\np <= alpha?      True\n|z| > critical? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the tail areas","text":"A picture of the p-value: the red areas are the two tails beyond +z and -z. Their combined area is the two-sided p-value, and they are tiny compared with the whole curve.","code":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = np.linspace(-4, 4, 400)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, stats.norm.pdf(x), color='black')\nfor side in (x >= abs(z), x <= -abs(z)):\n    plt.fill_between(x[side], stats.norm.pdf(x[side]), color='tab:red', alpha=0.5)\nplt.axvline(crit_two, linestyle='--', color='gray')\nplt.axvline(-crit_two, linestyle='--', color='gray')\nplt.title(f'z = {z:.2f}: two-sided p = {p_two:.4f} (red); dashed = critical values')\nplt.xlabel('z')\nplt.show()","explain":["Load matplotlib's plotting tools as plt. We keep this import here because only this step draws.","Make 400 evenly spaced z values from -4 to 4 to draw the curve smoothly.","Start a new, small figure (6 by 3.4 inches) so it fits on the page.","Draw the normal curve in black: its height at each z is stats.norm.pdf.","Loop over the two tail regions: z at or above +|z|, and at or below -|z|.","Shade each tail in red. The shaded area is the probability of being that extreme, so it is the p-value.","Draw a dashed line at the upper critical value, where the rejection region begins.","Draw the matching dashed line at the lower critical value.","Title the plot with the numbers so the picture explains itself.","Label the horizontal axis; z is measured in standard errors.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"The red tails beyond +2.40 and -2.40 together are the two-sided p of 0.0164, a very small share of the curve. Our z lies outside the dashed critical lines at +/-1.96, so it is in the rejection region. The picture shows p is an area, the share of outcomes this extreme, and not the probability that H0 is true.","out":"","figs":["6.2-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"xbar <- 503.6; mu0 <- 500; sigma <- 12; n <- 64\nz <- (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / sqrt(n))\nz\npnorm(z, lower.tail = FALSE)   # right-tailed p\npnorm(z)                       # left-tailed p\n2 * pnorm(-abs(z))             # two-tailed p\nqnorm(c(0.975, 0.95))          # critical values","explain":["Store the sample mean, the claimed mean, the known sigma and n on one line (semicolons separate commands in R).","Compute z: the gap divided by the standard error sigma / sqrt(n).","Typing the name z alone prints its value.","pnorm gives the area to the left; lower.tail = FALSE flips it to the right tail, the right-tailed p-value.","pnorm(z) is the area to the left of z, the left-tailed p-value.","Two tails: double the area below -|z|. This is the two-tailed p-value.","qnorm is the inverse of pnorm: it returns the critical z values for 0.975 (two-tailed 5%) and 0.95 (right-tailed 5%)."],"expect":"z = 2.4; right-tailed p = 0.0082; left-tailed p = 0.9918; two-tailed p = 0.0164; critical values 1.960 and 1.645","read":"z prints 2.4. The right-tailed p is 0.0082, the left-tailed 0.9918 and the two-tailed 0.0164. qnorm gives 1.959964 and 1.644854. For the question 'different from 500?' use 0.0164: it is below 0.05, so we reject H0.","note":"pnorm gives the area to the left, so lower.tail = FALSE gives the right tail. qnorm is the inverse and returns the critical values."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the CDF.NORMAL and IDF.NORMAL functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / xbar mu0 sigma n.\nBEGIN DATA\n503.6 500 12 64\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / SQRT(n)).\nCOMPUTE p_right = 1 - CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE p_left = CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE p_two = 2 * (1 - CDF.NORMAL(ABS(z), 0, 1)).\nCOMPUTE crit_two = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE crit_right = IDF.NORMAL(0.95, 0, 1).\nLIST.","explain":["Define four variables read in free format: the sample mean, claimed mean, sigma and n.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","One data row with the four values, in the same order as the variables.","Marks the end of the data.","Compute z = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / sqrt(n)), the gap in standard errors.","CDF.NORMAL gives the area to the left of z, so 1 minus it is the right-tailed p-value.","The area to the left of z is the left-tailed p-value.","Two-tailed p: twice the right-hand area beyond the absolute value of z.","IDF.NORMAL is the inverse curve: the z that leaves 2.5% in the top tail, the two-tailed critical value.","The z that leaves 5% in the top tail, the right-tailed critical value.","LIST prints all variables, so we can read z, the p-values and the critical values."],"read":"In the LIST output look at z (2.40, SPSS shows 2 decimals), p_right (0.01), p_left (0.99), p_two (0.02) and the critical values 1.96 and 1.64 (rounded display). The exact two-tailed p is 0.0164, below 0.05, so we reject H0.","note":"SPSS has no menu for a z-test with known sigma, so the numbers are computed with normal-curve functions. LIST prints z, the p-values and the critical values (shown with SPSS's default 2 decimals; double-click the output to see more)."},"stata":{"code":"display (503.6 - 500) / (12 / sqrt(64))\ndisplay 1 - normal(2.4)         // right-tailed p\ndisplay normal(2.4)             // left-tailed p\ndisplay 2 * (1 - normal(2.4))   // two-tailed p\ndisplay invnormal(0.975)        // two-tailed critical value\ndisplay invnormal(0.95)         // right-tailed critical value","explain":["display works as a calculator. This line computes z = (503.6 - 500) / (12 / sqrt(64)) = 2.4.","normal() is the area to the left of a z value, so 1 minus it is the right-tailed p-value for z = 2.4.","normal(2.4) itself is the left-tailed p-value.","Double the right-hand tail to get the two-tailed p-value.","invnormal() is the inverse: the z with 97.5% to its left, the two-tailed critical value.","The z with 95% to its left, the right-tailed critical value."],"read":"The first line shows 2.4, then the p-values .0081975 (right), .9918025 (left) and .01639 (two-tailed), and the critical values 1.959964 and 1.644854. Use the two-tailed 0.0164 for our question: below 0.05, so reject H0.","note":"normal() is the area to the left of z and invnormal() is its inverse. The first line computes z = 2.4, which is then typed into the other lines."},"conclusion":"The z statistic of 2.40 gives a two-sided p-value of 0.0164, below 0.05, so there is evidence that the mean fill differs from 500 mL, and the sample mean of 503.6 mL points to overfilling. Caution: the p-value does not say how big the problem is. A 3.6 mL gap is under 1% of a bottle, and whether it matters is a business decision.","practice":{"scenario":"A phone maker rates its battery at 12 hours of video playback. Engineers know from years of testing that the spread is sigma = 0.8 hours. A reviewer tests 25 phones and records the hours each lasts. Do the phones last LESS than the rated 12 hours on average? Use a one-sided z-test.","data":"hours = [11.6, 11.8, 11.4, 10.9, 11.2, 10.8, 11.6, 12.7, 11.2, 11.1, 12.0, 11.9, 11.7, 10.9, 11.6, 12.2, 10.5, 11.2, 10.1, 10.6, 10.1, 11.4, 10.6, 11.8, 11.7]","tasks":["State H0 and H1 (which tail?) and find the sample mean.","Compute the standard error and the z statistic.","Find the left-tailed p-value and the two-tailed p-value.","Compare z with the critical value for alpha = 0.05 and state the decision."],"hint":"This is the bottling lab with a left tail. Use norm.cdf(z) for the left-tailed p and norm.ppf(0.05) for the critical value.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nhours = [11.6, 11.8, 11.4, 10.9, 11.2, 10.8, 11.6, 12.7, 11.2, 11.1, 12.0, 11.9, 11.7, 10.9, 11.6, 12.2, 10.5, 11.2, 10.1, 10.6, 10.1, 11.4, 10.6, 11.8, 11.7]\nmu0, sigma = 12, 0.8\nxbar, n = np.mean(hours), len(hours)\nz = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / np.sqrt(n))\nprint(f'mean = {xbar:.3f}, z = {z:.3f}')\nprint(f'left-tailed p = {stats.norm.cdf(z):.1e}')\nprint(f'two-tailed p  = {2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z)):.1e}')\nprint(f'critical z (left, alpha 0.05) = {stats.norm.ppf(0.05):.3f}')\nprint('reject H0?', z < stats.norm.ppf(0.05))","answer":"The mean is 11.304 hours and z = -4.350. The left-tailed p is about 6.8e-06 (roughly 7 in a million), far below 0.05, and z is below the critical value -1.645, so we reject H0: the phones last less than 12 hours on average. Caution: the test assumes sigma = 0.8 is truly known, and the 0.7 hour shortfall is for you to judge, not the p-value.","out":"mean = 11.304, z = -4.350\nleft-tailed p = 6.8e-06\ntwo-tailed p  = 1.4e-05\ncritical z (left, alpha 0.05) = -1.645\nreject H0? True","figs":[]}}});
